  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 AM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Today will be last day with above average \ntemperatures for many days as a cold front marking the leading edge \nof a cooler and drier airmass moves across the region today shifting \nconvective coverage southward.\n\nA cold front across northern VA early this morning will drop south \ninto NC this morning and into SC tonight. A notably drier and cooler \nairmass will filter into NC this afternoon and especially tonight \nwith surface dew points dropping into the upper 50s and lower 60s \nlate tonight. While the airmass will begin to dry out from north to \nsouth this afternoon and especially tonight, there will be enough \nlingering low and mid level moisture to support a few showers this \nmorning, mainly across the Coastal Plain and eastern Piedmont. With \nheating this afternoon, MLCAPE values across the southern Piedmont, \nSandhills and southern Coastal Plain will briefly climb to around \n1000 J/Kg supporting scattered thunderstorms during the early and \nmid afternoon hours before drier air shifts the convection south \nduring the late afternoon. Dry weather is expected this evening and \novernight. Ahead of the arrival of the cooler airmass, high \ntemperatures will climb into the mid 80s in the Triad, the upper 80s \nin the Triangle and near 90 in the Sandhills. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A wavy frontal zone will buckle northward Sat and \nagain on Mon, but rainfall amounts appear limited.\n\nA high-latitude omega-blocking pattern will prevail through the \nforecast period with a highly anomalous and persistent mid/upper-lvl \nridge extending into central Canada which will be bookended by a \npair of mid/upper-lvl lows over CA and the Canadian maritimes. This \nstagnant pattern will favor a series of shortwaves to lock-in mean \ntroughing over the Northeast and off the Mid-Atlantic Coast and push \nseveral reinforcing cold fronts into the area potentially through \nTues.\n\nThe quasi-stationary boundary is expected to buckle northward a bit \non Sat but will likely remain well south of central NC. Deep layer \nmoisture will however be on the rise as the remnants of a mid-lvl \nshear axis lifts atop this boundary and into the Carolinas. This \nwill likely support mostly increased cloud cover and very light rain \nand/or virga as forcing for ascent will likely be disjointed. Some \nmoisture pooling ahead of the approaching cold frontal passage may \nsupport some isolated showers, but widespread deep convection \nappears unlikely.\n\nSome shallow moisture return ahead of yet another cold front appears \npossible on Mon as an area of surface high pressure shifts off the \nCarolina Coast and may result in isolated to scattered showers, but \nconfidence is low. Better chances will likely be over the upslope \nterrain in western NC with orographic forcing overcoming meager \ninstability. Localized enhancements to convective coverage appear \npossible along the track of any storm-scale MCVs, but predictability \non these meso-gamma to meso-beta features is nearly impossible with \nany degree of confidence at this time scale. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... A period of near to below average temperatures with \ngenerally fair and dry weather for much of the early to middle part \nof the upcoming work week. \n\nThe persistent upper level low across the Northeast and the \nCanadian maritimes will keep a northwest flow aloft across the \nCarolinas and mid-Atlantic. This will support a period of lower than \naverage heights and below average deep layer moisture featuring \ngenerally fair and dry weather with cooler than average \ntemperatures.",
